@@ -17,3 +17,20 @@ resolvePointer(doc, ""); // the whole document
 `pointer` is not a valid JSON Pointer (RFC 6901 §3), and an `Error` if it does not resolve to a value of
 the document: missing member, array index out of range, leading zeros, or `-` (§4). `null` is a value and
 resolves normally. Only own properties are followed, and the document is never modified.
+
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [{ op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" }]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+```
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies an array of operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`,
+`test`; paths are JSON Pointers) and returns the result as a new value; neither `document` nor `patch` is
+modified or shared with the result. It throws if the patch is invalid (not an array, malformed operation,
+unknown `op`, missing `path`/`value`/`from`, malformed pointer) or an operation fails (missing target, bad
+array index, failed `test`, `move` into its own child). Processing stops at the first error and nothing is
+returned, so a failed patch has no partial effect. `test` compares by JSON equality (member order is
+irrelevant). Removing the document root throws, as RFC 6902 leaves that case undefined. `__proto__` and
+similar keys are ordinary members.
